# Text whose 1kb paragraph repeats in the larger files, which therefore
# compress well.
#
+touch zero
+cat <<EOI >=1kb
A gzip file is a series of members, each of which holds the deflate data of
some original data between a header and a trailer. The header starts with
two identification bytes and the compression method, followed by the flags
that say which of the optional fields are present, the modification time of
the original data, the extra flags of the compression method, and the
operating system on which the compression took place. The optional fields
are an extra field of subfields, the original file name, a file comment, and
a CRC16 of the header itself. The trailer holds the CRC-32 of the original
data and its size modulo 2^32, which the decompressor verifies once it has
reached the end of the deflate data. The members of a file simply follow one
another, with nothing before, between, or after them, and the data of the
file is the data of all of its members, one after another. A decompressor
must check the identification bytes and the compression method, skip the
optional fields, and reject a member whose reserved flag bits are set, since
they may announce a field it doesn't know how to skip over.
EOI
+cat  1kb  1kb  1kb  1kb  1kb  1kb  1kb  1kb >=8kb
+cat  8kb  8kb  8kb  8kb  8kb  8kb  8kb  8kb >=64kb

w = $~

# The member tests compress at level 0, which stores the data as it is, so
# that we know every byte of the member. The header tests compress at level 6
# and only look at the header. So the header without any options is,
# respectively:
#
# 1f8b 08 00 00000000 04 ff
# 1f8b 08 00 00000000 00 ff
#
# That is, no flags, no time stamp, the extra flags of the level (fastest for
# level 0, none for level 6), and the unknown operating system.

: member
: Compress data into a whole member
:
{{
  : text
  : Member of 'hello' in a final stored block, with its CRC-32 and size
  :
  $* -c 0 <:'hello' | $* -x >>EOO
  1f8b08000000000004ff010500faff68656c6c6f86a6103605000000
  EOO

  : empty
  :
  $* -c 0 <:'' | $* -x >>EOO
  1f8b08000000000004ff010000ffff0000000000000000
  EOO
}}

: flags
: Set the extra flags from the level
:
{{
  $* -c 0 <:'' | $* -x 10 >'1f8b08000000000004ff' : 0
  $* -c 1 <:'' | $* -x 10 >'1f8b08000000000004ff' : 1
  $* -c 2 <:'' | $* -x 10 >'1f8b08000000000000ff' : 2
  $* -c 8 <:'' | $* -x 10 >'1f8b08000000000000ff' : 8
  $* -c 9 <:'' | $* -x 10 >'1f8b08000000000002ff' : 9

  : ignored
  : Extra flags of the header, which the level overrides
  :
  $* -c 6 --flags 2 <:'' | $* -x 10 >'1f8b08000000000000ff'
}}

: header
: Write the values and optional fields of the header
:
{{
  : text
  :
  $* -c 6 --text <:'' | $* -x 10 >'1f8b08010000000000ff'

  : mtime
  :
  $* -c 6 --mtime 1700000000 <:'' | $* -x 10 >'1f8b080000f1536500ff'

  : os
  :
  $* -c 6 --os 3 <:'' | $* -x 10 >'1f8b0800000000000003'

  : extra
  : Extra field with subfields 'AP' and 'xy'
  :
  $* -c 6 --extra '4150:0102,7879:' <:'' | $* -x 22 >>EOO
  1f8b08040000000000ff0a0041500200010278790000
  EOO

  : extra-empty
  : Extra field without subfields
  :
  $* -c 6 --extra '' <:'' | $* -x 12 >'1f8b08040000000000ff0000'

  : name
  :
  $* -c 6 --name 612e747874 <:'' | $* -x 16 >'1f8b08080000000000ff612e74787400'

  : name-empty
  : File name with nothing but the terminating zero byte
  :
  $* -c 6 --name '' <:'' | $* -x 11 >'1f8b08080000000000ff00'

  : comment
  : File comment of two lines
  :
  $* -c 6 --comment 6c696e65310a6c696e6532 <:'' | $* -x 22 >>EOO
  1f8b08100000000000ff6c696e65310a6c696e653200
  EOO

  : crc16
  :
  $* -c 6 --crc16 <:'' | $* -x 12 >'1f8b08020000000000ff90c9'

  : all
  : All of the optional fields, in the order of the header
  :
  $* -c 6 --extra '4150:0102' --name 68656c6c6f2e747874 \
     --comment 6772656574696e67 --crc16 <:'' | $* -x 39 >>EOO
  1f8b081e0000000000ff060041500200010268656c6c6f2e747874006772656574696e670051e4
  EOO

  : extra-max
  : Extra field of the largest size
  :
  $* -c 6 --pad 65531 <:'hello' | $* -d >:'hello'
}}

: invalid
: Test headers that the format doesn't allow
:
{{
  : name
  : File name with a zero byte
  :
  $* -c 6 --name 6100 <:'' 2>>EOE != 0
  error: file name contains zero byte
  EOE

  : comment
  : File comment with a zero byte
  :
  $* -c 6 --comment 0061 <:'' 2>>EOE != 0
  error: file comment contains zero byte
  EOE

  : subfield-id
  : Subfield with a reserved ID
  :
  $* -c 6 --extra '4100:' <:'' 2>>EOE != 0
  error: extra subfield with reserved ID
  EOE

  : extra-long
  : Extra field longer than the format allows
  :
  $* -c 6 --pad 65532 <:'' 2>>EOE != 0
  error: extra field longer than 65535 bytes
  EOE
}}

: round-trip
: Compress data and decompress the result
:
{{
  $* -c 6 <<<$w/zero | $* -d >>>$w/zero : zero
  $* -c 6 <<<$w/1kb  | $* -d >>>$w/1kb  : 1kb
  $* -c 6 <<<$w/64kb | $* -d >>>$w/64kb : 64kb
  $* -c 0 <<<$w/64kb | $* -d >>>$w/64kb : store
  $* -c 9 <<<$w/64kb | $* -d >>>$w/64kb : smallest

  : header
  : Data with all of the optional fields of the header
  :
  $* -c 6 --extra '4150:0102' --name 68656c6c6f2e747874 \
     --comment 6772656574696e67 --crc16 <<<$w/8kb | $* -d >>>$w/8kb
}}

: chunk
: Compress in parts, which gives the same data as in one go
:
{{
  : byte
  :
  $* -c 6 --name 612e747874 --crc16 <<<$w/8kb >=whole;
  $* -c 6 --name 612e747874 --crc16 --chunk 1 <<<$w/8kb >>>whole

  : text
  :
  $* -c 9 <<<$w/64kb >=whole;
  $* -c 9 --chunk 7 <<<$w/64kb >>>whole
}}

: reset
: Compress again after a reset, which gives the same data
:
$* -c 6 --name 612e747874 <<<$w/8kb >=whole;
$* -c 6 --name 612e747874 --reset <<<$w/8kb >>>whole
